<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>vocabulary</title><style>table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #333;padding:6px}th{background:#eee}img{max-width:100%}</style></head><body><h1>vocabulary</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Synonyms and Antonyms</li><li>Vocabulary: Word Formation, Idioms, and Phrases</li></ul><h2>Synonyms and Antonyms</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Are Synonyms and Antonyms?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Synonyms are words that have the same or similar meanings in certain contexts (e.g., big/large, happy/joyful, smart/intelligent). Antonyms are words with opposite meanings (e.g., hot/cold, brave/cowardly, abundant/scarse). In the NEB Class 11 English examination, synonym and antonym questions typically carry 3-6 marks and appear in objective (MCQ) and subjective (one-word substitution) formats. Mastering synonyms and antonyms is essential for vocabulary expansion, improving writing quality, and performing well in comprehension passages where precise word choice matters.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Types of Synonyms</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Synonyms can be classified into several types: Absolute synonyms — words that are interchangeable in all contexts (rare in English — &#x27;lift&#x27; and &#x27;elevator&#x27; are close but used in different registers). Relative synonyms — words with similar meanings but different connotations, registers, or collocations (e.g., &#x27;slim&#x27; vs. &#x27;thin&#x27; vs. &#x27;skinny&#x27; — all mean &#x27;not fat&#x27; but with different emotional overtones). Contextual synonyms — words that are synonyms only in specific contexts (e.g., &#x27;start&#x27; and &#x27;begin&#x27; are synonyms when referring to an action, but not when &#x27;start&#x27; means &#x27;engine ignition&#x27;). NEB exams typically test relative and contextual synonyms.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Types of Antonyms</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Antonyms can be classified into three types: Gradable antonyms — words that exist on a spectrum and can be modified (hot/cold — something can be very hot or slightly cold). Complementary antonyms — words that are absolute opposites with no middle ground (dead/alive, present/absent, true/false). Converse antonyms — words that express a reciprocal relationship (buy/sell, teacher/student, above/below). Understanding these types helps students choose the correct antonym in context. NEB exams may ask students to identify antonyms from a list or to choose the best opposite for a word in a given sentence.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Strategies for Learning Synonyms and Antonyms</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Effective strategies include: (1) Learning in context — study words as they appear in sentences, not in isolation. Context reveals the appropriate register and connotation. (2) Word families — learn related words together (happy, happiness, happily, unhappy, unhappy, happiness). This builds understanding of word formation and relationship. (3) Flashcards — write the word on one side, synonyms/antonyms and example sentences on the other. (4) Reading widely — exposure to diverse texts naturally builds vocabulary. (5) Using a thesaurus wisely — a thesaurus lists synonyms but does not explain connotations — always check usage in context. (6) Regular review — spaced repetition is essential for long-term retention.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Common Synonym/Antonym Patterns in NEB Exams</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>NEB exams frequently test the following word pairs and patterns: Abstract nouns from adjectives : happy → happiness, sad → sadness, kind → kindness. Opposite prefixes : happy/unhappy, possible/impossible, careful/careless, honest/dishonest. Contextual meaning : The word &#x27;bright&#x27; has different antonyms depending on context — &#x27;bright light&#x27; (antonym: dim) vs. &#x27;bright student&#x27; (antonym: dull) vs. &#x27;bright future&#x27; (antonym: bleak). NEB exams sometimes test this contextual sensitivity by providing a sentence and asking for the appropriate synonym or antonym.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Avoiding Common Mistakes</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Common errors in synonym/antonym questions: (1) Choosing a word with the wrong connotation — &#x27;slim&#x27; and &#x27;fat&#x27; are antonyms, but &#x27;skinny&#x27; and &#x27;fat&#x27; are not appropriate in formal contexts. (2) Ignoring context — choosing a synonym that fits the dictionary definition but not the specific sentence. (3) Confusing synonyms with related words — &#x27;result&#x27; and &#x27;consequence&#x27; are related but not perfect synonyms. (4) Choosing antonyms from the wrong category — &#x27;hot&#x27; and &#x27;cold&#x27; are gradable antonyms; &#x27;hot&#x27; and &#x27;spicy&#x27; are not antonyms (spicy describes taste, hot describes temperature). (5) Using words that are too extreme — the antonym of &#x27;small&#x27; is &#x27;large&#x27;, not &#x27;enormous&#x27; (which implies a different degree). Always choose the most precise and context-appropriate word.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Synonym vs. Equivalent: A synonym is a single word with nearly the same meaning as another word in most contexts (big ≈ large). An equivalent may be a phrase or expression that conveys the same idea (very big ≈ enormous). In NEB exams, synonym questions ask for a single-word replacement. One-word substitution is related but more specific — it provides a definition and asks for the single word that matches it.</td></tr><tr><td>Antonym vs. Opposite: An antonym is a specific vocabulary term for a word with the opposite meaning (hot/cold, brave/cowardly). An &#x27;opposite&#x27; is a broader concept that includes antonyms but also refers to anything contrary. In NEB exams, use &#x27;antonym&#x27; when referring to vocabulary relationships. Some words have multiple antonyms depending on context — &#x27;light&#x27; (weight) → antonym: heavy; &#x27;light&#x27; (illumination) → antonym: dark.</td></tr><tr><td>Contextual synonym vs. Absolute synonym: Absolute synonyms (words interchangeable in ALL contexts) are extremely rare in English. Most so-called synonyms are contextual — they are interchangeable only in specific situations. For example, &#x27;begin&#x27; and &#x27;start&#x27; are often synonyms, but &#x27;start the car&#x27; is correct while &#x27;begin the car&#x27; is not. NEB exams sometimes test this distinction by providing a sentence where only one of two similar words is appropriate.</td></tr><tr><td>Gradable vs. Complementary antonyms: Gradable antonyms (hot/cold, big/small) exist on a continuum — something can be more or less of either. Complementary antonyms (dead/alive, present/absent) are absolute opposites — something cannot be both or neither. This distinction matters in NEB exams because it affects how antonyms are used in sentences. For example, &#x27;not hot&#x27; does not necessarily mean &#x27;cold&#x27; (it could be warm), but &#x27;not dead&#x27; necessarily means &#x27;alive&#x27; (in the complementary pair).</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Vocabulary: Word Formation, Idioms, and Phrases</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Word Formation</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Word formation is the process of creating new words from existing words using affixes (prefixes and suffixes), compounding, conversion, or blending. In the NEB Class 11 English examination, word formation questions (typically 3-5 marks) ask students to change a given word into a different part of speech or to fill in a blank with the correct derived form. Prefixes are added to the beginning of a word (un-, dis-, re-, pre-, mis-). Suffixes are added to the end ( -ness, -ment, -tion, -able, -ful, -less, -ly, -er, -ist). Understanding word formation patterns is essential for vocabulary expansion and grammar accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Common Prefixes and Their Meanings</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Key prefixes tested in NEB exams: un- (not, opposite): unhappy, unlock, unfair. dis- (not, opposite, removal): disagree, disappear, dishonest. re- (again): rewrite, rebuild, reinstall. pre- (before): prehistoric, prepaid, preview. mis- (wrongly): misunderstand, mislead, misplace. in-/im-/il-/ir- (not): impossible, illegal, irregular, incomplete. anti- (against): antibiotic, anti-social. semi- (half): semifinal, semicircle. sub- (under): submarine, submodule. over- (too much): overwork, overeat. under- (too little): underpaid, underestimate. Memorising these prefixes helps in solving word formation and vocabulary questions efficiently.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Common Suffixes and Their Functions</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Key suffixes tested in NEB exams: -ness (forms nouns from adjectives): happiness, kindness, darkness. -ment (forms nouns from verbs): achievement, agreement, development. -tion/-sion (forms nouns from verbs): education, decision, admission. -able/-ible (forms adjectives from verbs): readable, visible, accessible. -ful (forms adjectives from nouns): helpful, beautiful, careless. -less (forms adjectives meaning &#x27;without&#x27;): hopeless, fearless, endless. -ly (forms adverbs from adjectives): quickly, carefully, happily. -er/-or (forms nouns indicating doer): teacher, actor, writer. -ist (forms nouns indicating specialist): artist, scientist, pessimist. -ity (forms nouns from adjectives): ability, purity, activity. -ous/-ious (forms adjectives from nouns): dangerous, nervous, famous.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Idioms: Fixed Expressions with Figurative Meaning</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Idioms are fixed expressions whose meaning cannot be deduced from the literal meanings of individual words. Examples: &#x27;Break the ice&#x27; = to initiate conversation in a social setting. &#x27;Hit the nail on the head&#x27; = to describe exactly what is causing a situation or problem. &#x27;A dime a dozen&#x27; = very common and not valuable. &#x27;Bite the bullet&#x27; = to face a difficult situation courageously. &#x27;Cost an arm and a leg&#x27; = to be very expensive. &#x27;Let the cat out of the bag&#x27; = to reveal a secret accidentally. &#x27;Beat around the bush&#x27; = to avoid talking about something directly. &#x27;Once in a blue moon&#x27; = very rarely. Idioms are extensively tested in NEB exams through gap-filling, sentence completion, and meaning-matching questions.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Common Phrasal Verbs</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Phrasal verbs are combinations of a verb + particle (preposition or adverb) that create a new meaning. Examples: give up = stop trying / surrender. look after = take care of. run out of = use all of something. put off = postpone. turn up = appear / arrive. break down = stop working (machine) / lose control (emotion). carry on = continue. get along = have a good relationship. come across = find by chance. figure out = understand / solve. Phrasal verbs are one of the most challenging aspects of English vocabulary for Nepali students, as Nepali does not have an equivalent particle system.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>One-Word Substitution</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>One-word substitution replaces a phrase or group of words with a single word that has the same meaning. Common examples tested in NEB exams: Belonging to one state only = Native. Love of books = Bibliophile. Study of words = Lexicography. Love of humanity = Philanthropist. That can be felt = Sensible. That cannot be felt = Insensible. A person who knows many languages = Polyglot. A person who loves peace = Pacifist. That cannot be destroyed = Indestructible. A short, witty remark = Epigram. One-word substitution questions typically carry 2-4 marks in NEB exams and require extensive vocabulary preparation.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Idiom vs. Phrasal verb: An idiom is a fixed expression whose meaning is figurative and unpredictable from its parts (&#x27;break the ice&#x27; ≠ literally breaking ice). A phrasal verb is a verb + particle combination where the meaning may be literal or figurative (&#x27;give up&#x27; can mean &#x27;stop&#x27; (figurative) or &#x27;hand over&#x27; (literal)). All phrasal verbs are not idioms, and not all idioms are phrasal verbs.</td></tr><tr><td>Synonym vs. Equivalent: A synonym is a word with nearly the same meaning as another in most contexts (&#x27;big&#x27; and &#x27;large&#x27;). An equivalent may be a phrase that expresses the same idea (&#x27;very big&#x27; ≈ &#x27;enormous&#x27;). In NEB exams, synonym questions typically ask for a single-word replacement, not a phrase. One-word substitution is related but more specific — it replaces a definition with a single word.</td></tr><tr><td>Prefix vs. Suffix: A prefix is added to the BEGINNING of a word (un-happy, re-write, mis-lead). A suffix is added to the END of a word (happi-ness, write-r, lead-er). Both change the meaning or grammatical function of the base word. Confusing prefix with suffix is a common error in word formation questions.</td></tr><tr><td>Connotation vs. Denotation: Denotation is the literal, dictionary definition of a word (&#x27;home&#x27; = a place where one lives). Connotation is the emotional or cultural association of a word (&#x27;home&#x27; = warmth, safety, family). Two words may have the same denotation but different connotations (&#x27;thin&#x27; vs. &#x27;slim&#x27; vs. &#x27;skinny&#x27;). NEB exams sometimes test this distinction in vocabulary questions asking for the &#x27;best&#x27; word choice.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Synonyms and Antonyms</td><td>Synonyms and Antonyms covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Vocabulary: Word Formation, Idioms, and Phrases</td><td>Vocabulary: Word Formation, Idioms, and Phrases covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr></table></body></html>